        "content": "## PROF1: Professional Behavior — Level 3 / 5 · Competent\nPediatric Critical Care milestone · levels 1–5, novice → expert (Dreyfus)\n\n**Assigned level:** 3 · Competent — the highest level fully demonstrated. The ceiling here is set jointly by a learner shortfall (no explicit anticipatory-intervention behavior for Level 4's first criterion) and by the simulation's scope (Level 5's community/specialty engagement cannot arise in a bedside consent conversation).\n\n**Level progression:** Levels 1–3 demonstrated; Level 4 partially demonstrated; Level 5 not assessable in this simulation.\n\n## Evidence by level\n\n### Level 1 · Novice — Demonstrated\nThe learner exhibits expected professional behaviors for an informed-consent discussion and shows an understanding of the intensivist's role in weighing risks and benefits for the patient.\n\"in everything we do in medicine, and, uh, especially everything we do in a pediatric ICU, has a risk and benefit. Um, seldom is there only one right way to proceed.\"\n\"we do, as a team, assess every day whether or not a child truly needs that line to minimize the risk of injury to the surrounding structure and infection.\"\n\n### Level 2 · Advanced beginner — Demonstrated\nProfessional behavior is sustained throughout with no observable lapses, and the learner takes clear accountability for the care being proposed, including personally naming their role in the procedure.\n\"I would be the one placing the line, and then, uh, with me in the room, there would also be an ICU attending who was responsible solely for supervising me and providing direction.\"\n\"It's, it's part of both the nurses' and the doc- the doctor's job multiple times through a shift to assess how an IV line or a central venous line is working.\"\n\n### Level 3 · Competent — Demonstrated\nThe learner maintains professionalism through an emotionally charged, complex conversation with an anxious parent, volunteering risks transparently rather than minimizing them, and fully engages with the family's questions and fears. When the parent's audio dropped repeatedly, the learner consistently paused and re-checked rather than talking over her.\n\"if it's okay with you, I do wanna, um, talk about one other risk, in full transparency, of a central venous catheter.\"\n\"I would be the one placing the line\" — full ownership of the procedure.\n\"I, I wish I had a better, more direct answer, but I, I don't really know right now\" — honest acknowledgment of uncertainty rather than a false reassurance.\n\n### Level 4 · Proficient — Partially demonstrated\nLevel 4 requires (a) recognizing situations that may trigger professionalism lapses and intervening to prevent lapses in self and others, and (b) exhibiting a sense of duty to patient care and professional responsibilities. The learner clearly demonstrated the second criterion — a strong sense of duty, prioritizing patient comfort, transparency, and daily reassessment. The first criterion is not demonstrated: the learner had no occasion (and took no action) involving a colleague's lapse, and did not verbalize any self-monitoring of a trigger. The scenario stayed collaborative, so the intervention behavior did not arise.\n\"we take patient comfort exceptionally seriously. In the pediatric ICU.\" — duty to the patient.\n\"Thank you for being so honest with me\" (character response) reflects the duty and transparency the learner sustained.\n\n### Level 5 · Expert — Not assessable in this simulation (no opportunity)\nLevel 5 turns on modeling behavior for and coaching others, and on extending the intensivist's role beyond direct patient care into the community, specialty, and profession. A single bedside consent conversation with one parent offers no occasion to coach a colleague or engage the wider profession, so this level cannot be surfaced here; its absence does not count against the learner.\n\n## Rationale\nThe learner met every Level 1–3 behavior. They identified expected professional conduct and the risk/benefit framing central to intensive care (Level 1), took personal accountability and named their supervised role and the team's shared responsibility for monitoring the line (Level 2), and — most notably — sustained professionalism through a lengthy, emotionally loaded conversation with a frightened parent, repeatedly checking that she was following, volunteering the pneumothorax and clot risks in \"full transparency\" rather than glossing them, and admitting the limits of their knowledge honestly (Level 3). Fully engaged, honest, and accountable throughout.\n\n## Why not the next level\nLevel 4's defining behaviors are (a) recognizing lapse-triggering situations and intervening to prevent lapses in self and others, and (b) a sense of duty. The learner clearly showed the duty component but never demonstrated the intervention/coaching-adjacent behavior — no colleague lapse arose to address, and the learner did not verbalize noticing a stressor and self-correcting. That behavior was largely not surfaced by a cooperative, single-family scenario (no opportunity), though the learner also did not create an opening for it. Level 5's coaching and community/specialty engagement simply cannot occur in a bedside consent encounter (no opportunity). The assessment therefore holds at Level 3.\n\n## Recommendations\nTo reach Level 4 in an assessable way, narrate anticipatory professionalism when stressors appear — for example, when the parent's audio kept dropping, a line like: \"I want to slow down and make sure I'm not rushing you through something this important — please stop me any time.\" This makes visible that you are monitoring a trigger (an overwhelmed parent, a long high-stakes conversation) and actively guarding against a lapse in your own communication. In team settings, look for moments to model transparency for junior members and to gently redirect when a colleague's communication falls short. Continue the strengths already shown: proactively volunteering risks, admitting uncertainty honestly, and pausing to confirm understanding.\n\n## Overall impression\n- **Strengths** — Sustained genuine professionalism through a long, emotionally charged consent conversation: transparent about serious risks (pneumothorax, clot) without being asked, honest about the limits of prognostication, personally accountable for the procedure and role, and consistently patient when the parent's audio cut out, re-checking rather than steamrolling.\n- **Priority gaps** — Did not demonstrate the Level 4 anticipatory behavior of naming a lapse-triggering situation and intervening in oneself or others; this was partly a function of a cooperative single-family scenario that gave no colleague-coaching opening.\n- **Top action items**\n  1. Verbalize self-monitoring under stress (e.g., \"let me slow down so I don't rush you\") to make anticipatory professionalism observable.\n  2. In team-based encounters, seek chances to model and, where appropriate, coach professional communication in others.\n  3. Keep doing what you did well — volunteer risks proactively, own your role, and confirm the family's understanding before moving on.",
